.TH cirrocast 1  "cirrocast 1.3.0" 
.SH NAME
cirrocast \- Terminal weather client with pluggable backends and wttr.in\-style output
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcirrocast\fR [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-tz\fR] [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-provider\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-format\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-days\fR] [\fB\-u\fR|\fB\-\-units\fR] [\fB\-\-lang\fR] [\fB\-\-lat\fR] [\fB\-\-lon\fR] [\fB\-\-ip\fR] [\fB\-\-station\fR] [\fB\-\-alerts\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-alerts\fR] [\fB\-\-alerts\-from\fR] [\fB\-\-severity\fR] [\fB\-\-aqi\fR] [\fB\-\-aqi\-index\fR] [\fB\-\-moon\fR] [\fB\-\-date\fR] [\fB\-\-history\fR] [\fB\-\-marine\fR] [\fB\-\-normals\fR] [\fB\-\-template\fR] [\fB\-\-template\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-width\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR] [\fB\-\-refresh\fR] [\fB\-\-offline\fR] [\fB\-\-pick\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fILOCATION\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Terminal weather client with pluggable backends and wttr.in\-style output
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print more detail: settings, resolution notes, the upstream request behind the answer (secrets redacted) and the error cause chain. Repeat (`\-vv`) for every HTTP attempt, its status, and the cache decisions as well
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress non\-essential output
.TP
\fB\-\-tz\fR \fI<ZONE>\fR
Express the report\*(Aqs times in this IANA zone (e.g. `Asia/Shanghai`) instead of the zone the location resolves; overrides `[location] tz`
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-provider\fR \fI<LIST>\fR
Provider chain, comma separated; `auto` ranks the keyless backends by coverage for the resolved place (plus `metar` with `\-\-station`)
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Output format: a built\-in name (`art\-table`, `one\-line`, `plain`, `json`, `dumb`, `alerts`, `aqi`, `moon`, `normals`), a one\-line preset (`default`, `short`, `minimal`, `full`, `uv`, `sun`) or a `[templates]` key
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-days\fR \fI<N>\fR
Forecast days, `0` = current conditions only (clamped to what the provider serves)
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-units\fR \fI<SYSTEM>\fR
Unit system for the rendered values: `metric`, `us` or `uk`. The JSON format always emits canonical metric, whatever this says
.TP
\fB\-\-lang\fR \fI<TAG>\fR
Output language as a BCP\-47 tag, or `auto`
.TP
\fB\-\-lat\fR \fI<DEG>\fR
Latitude in degrees, north positive; needs `\-\-lon`, and replaces the location argument
.TP
\fB\-\-lon\fR \fI<DEG>\fR
Longitude in degrees, east positive; needs `\-\-lat`
.TP
\fB\-\-ip\fR
Locate from the public IP address. This sends the address to ipwho.is (falling back to ipapi.co and IP.SB); the answer is cached for 24 hours. It never happens without `\-\-ip` or an empty location everywhere
.TP
\fB\-\-station\fR \fI<ICAO>\fR
METAR station identifier (ICAO, four characters); `metar` heads the provider chain
.TP
\fB\-\-alerts\fR
Fetch severe\-weather warnings: the sources covering the location are selected automatically (national services first, the global aggregators after). Also on by default unless `[alerts] enabled = false`; `\-\-no\-alerts` turns it off for one run
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-alerts\fR
Do not fetch severe\-weather warnings in this run
.TP
\fB\-\-alerts\-from\fR \fI<LIST>\fR
Alert sources to query instead of the coverage\-selected set, comma separated: `nws`, `meteoalarm`, `qweather`, `hko`, `wmoswic`, `fpas`, `visualcrossing`. A source that does not cover the location is refused, and `visualcrossing` needs `\-\-provider visualcrossing` because its warnings travel in that payload
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fI<LEVEL>\fR
Lowest alert severity to show (`unknown`, `minor`, `moderate`, `severe`, `extreme`); overrides `[alerts] severity_threshold`
.TP
\fB\-\-aqi\fR
Append the air\-quality panel (US and European AQI, the six pollutants, pollen where the source covers it and the report\*(Aqs UV reading) to the table and plain output, and carry it as a typed object in `json`. `\-\-format aqi` prints the panel standalone
.TP
\fB\-\-aqi\-index\fR \fI<SCALE>\fR
Which AQI scale drives the panel\*(Aqs colour and the one\-line `%q` token: `us` or `european`; overrides `[air] index`. Needs `\-\-aqi` or `\-\-format aqi`
.TP
\fB\-\-moon\fR
Compute the moon and sun block locally (no request) and append it to the table and `plain` output; `json` then carries it as the `astro` object. `\-\-format moon` prints the standalone view, and `one\-line` shows the moon through the `%m`/`%M` tokens
.TP
\fB\-\-date\fR \fI<YYYY\-MM\-DD>\fR
Render the archive for one date (`YYYY\-MM\-DD`) instead of a forecast; needs a history\-capable backend
.TP
\fB\-\-history\fR \fI<Nd>\fR
Render the archive for the last `<N>d` days, ending yesterday; needs a history\-capable backend
.TP
\fB\-\-marine\fR
Append the marine block (waves, swell, sea\-surface temperature)
.TP
\fB\-\-normals\fR
Compare the forecast with the climate: fetch the month\*(Aqs normal for the station nearest the location from NOAA NCEI\*(Aqs Global Summary of the Month (two extra requests, cached for 30 days) and append the comparison to the table and `plain` output, or carry it as the typed `normals` object in `json`. `\-\-format normals` prints it standalone. Off unless asked for, or unless `[defaults] normals = true`
.TP
\fB\-\-template\fR \fI<TEMPLATE>\fR
Template for a one\-line output: a literal `%`\-token string, or `@PRESET`. The presets are `@default`, `@short`, `@minimal`, `@full`, `@uv` and `@sun`, plus any `[templates]` key; `\-\-help` lists every token and the width/precision syntax. An unknown token is a usage error. Needs `\-\-format one\-line` (or a format name that selects no template itself)
.TP
\fB\-\-template\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Read the one\-line template from a file; `\-` reads standard input. Same restrictions as `\-\-template`
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<WHEN>\fR
When to colour the output
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: Colour when stdout is a terminal that supports it
.IP \(bu 2
always: Colour even when piping
.IP \(bu 2
never: Never colour
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-width\fR \fI<COLS>\fR
Layout width in columns for the table formats (1..=500; below 20 is raised to it)
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<SECS>\fR
Per\-request timeout in seconds; overrides `network.timeout_secs`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR
Ignore the cache for this run and store nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-refresh\fR
Ignore cached answers and replace them with fresh ones
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR[=\fI<MODE>\fR]
Run offline: `=weather` serves the weather from the cache only, `=geo` resolves names from the bundled city table only, and bare `\-\-offline` (same as `=all`) never opens a socket. Overrides `[network] offline`
.TP
\fB\-\-pick\fR
Ask which candidate to use when a location name matches several places, instead of taking the ranked winner. The ranked list goes to stderr and one line is read from stdin
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Take the ranked winner when a location name matches several places, without asking. This is the default on a non\-terminal run; the flag makes that explicit and keeps a prompt from ever appearing
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fILOCATION\fR]
Location arguments (`Beijing`, `:Beijing`, `~Tsinghua`, `@39.9,116.4`, `@home`); several arguments are fetched at most four at a time and printed in argument order. Omitted = the configured default location, else the public IP
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
cirrocast\-config(1)
Inspect and edit the configuration file
.TP
cirrocast\-key(1)
Manage API keys for the key\-requiring providers
.TP
cirrocast\-provider(1)
Inspect the supported weather providers
.TP
cirrocast\-location(1)
Resolve a location argument without fetching weather
.TP
cirrocast\-cache(1)
Inspect and maintain the on\-disk cache
.TP
cirrocast\-status(1)
Print one line for a status bar: never aborts on a network failure
.TP
cirrocast\-completion(1)
Print a shell completion script
.TP
cirrocast\-man(1)
Print the manual page as roff
.TP
cirrocast\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
.SH EXTRA
CONFIG PRECEDENCE (highest first)
  command line flag > CIRROCAST_* environment variable > config.toml > built\-in default
  \-\-provider  CIRROCAST_PROVIDER   \-\-days    CIRROCAST_DAYS     \-\-timeout CIRROCAST_TIMEOUT
  \-\-format    CIRROCAST_FORMAT     \-\-units   CIRROCAST_UNITS    LOCATION  CIRROCAST_LOCATION
  \-\-lang      CIRROCAST_LANG       \-\-tz      CIRROCAST_TZ
  env only    CIRROCAST_LOCATION_PICK  CIRROCAST_NOMINATIM_URL  CIRROCAST_IP_SERVICE
              CIRROCAST_GEO_SEARCH  CIRROCAST_GEO_REVERSE  CIRROCAST_GEONAMES_USER
              CIRROCAST_NORMALS_PERIOD  CIRROCAST_NORMALS_MAX_DISTANCE_KM
  The configuration file is consulted only when neither the flag nor the variable is set, so an
  environment value is never overridden by config.toml. `config get <key>` prints the variable\*(Aqs
  value when one is set, and `config validate` checks the file without touching the network.

ONE\-LINE TOKENS (\-\-format one\-line, full, minimal, or a [templates] key)
  %c condition art    %C condition text   %x condition, plain text
  %t temp             %f feels\-like       %H today\*(Aqs high    %L today\*(Aqs low
  %w wind             %h humidity         %p precip          %P pressure
  %e dew point        %u UV index         %U UV + band       %m moon glyph
  %M moon phase       %v visibility       %l name            %d ISO date
  %D Wed 30 Sep       %T 15:04            %Z zone name       %z +0800
  %S sunrise          %s sunset           %q air\-quality index
  %A strongest alert event, empty when no alerts are in force
  %[\-][0][width][.prec]X pads (right with \-, zero for numbers); .prec truncates text and rounds
  numbers; %% prints one %; %{...} verbatim unless one token letter; \\n \\t \\\\ escapes; bad %X is exit 2.
  Presets (@NAME, and \-\-format NAME), listed with their templates:
    @default  %l: %c %C %t (%f), %w, %h, %p, %P, %v   @short  %c %t
    @minimal  %c%t   @uv  %l: UV %U   @sun  %l: sunrise %S sunset %s (%z %Z)
    @full     %l: %c %C %t (%f) %w %h %p %P %m %v %u %S %s %Z

MULTI\-LOCATION RUNS
  Several LOCATION arguments are fetched at most four at a time and printed in argument order.
  A failed location keeps its slot on stdout (`error: <query>: <message>`) and the run exits with
  the largest mapped code among the failures; `json` becomes an array, and `art\-table` draws a
  combined summary for up to four locations. CIRROCAST_LOCATION names one location — the environment
  tier has no second argument — so a multi\-location run passes its locations on the command line.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
success
.TP
\fB1\fR
generic failure: an unexpected error outside the classes below
.TP
\fB2\fR
usage: a flag or value the command line rejects, or mutually exclusive flags
.TP
\fB3\fR
network or upstream failure: no connection, a retryable status, an unusable body
.TP
\fB4\fR
configuration or state on disk: config.toml, keys.toml, the cache
.TP
\fB5\fR
location not found: an unresolvable name or station
.TP
\fB6\fR
missing or invalid API key: `cirrocast key set <id>` (or CIRROCAST_<ID>_KEY) fixes it
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBFlag overrides\fR
CIRROCAST_PROVIDER, CIRROCAST_FORMAT, CIRROCAST_UNITS, CIRROCAST_DAYS, CIRROCAST_LANG, CIRROCAST_LOCATION and CIRROCAST_TIMEOUT override the matching flag; a flag wins over the variable, and the variable over the configuration file.
.TP
\fBVariable\-only settings\fR
CIRROCAST_LOCATION_PICK, CIRROCAST_NOMINATIM_URL, CIRROCAST_IP_SERVICE, CIRROCAST_GEO_SEARCH, CIRROCAST_GEO_REVERSE, CIRROCAST_GEONAMES_USER, CIRROCAST_NORMALS_PERIOD and CIRROCAST_NORMALS_MAX_DISTANCE_KM have no flag; the configuration reference names the key each one overrides.
.TP
\fBProvider credentials\fR
CIRROCAST_<ID>_KEY holds a provider\*(Aqs API key, as an alternative to `key set <id>` and keys.toml.
.TP
\fBColour and terminal\fR
NO_COLOR, CLICOLOR_FORCE, TERM and COLORTERM drive colour and terminal\-capability detection; NO_COLOR is honoured unless CLICOLOR_FORCE asks for colour.
.TP
\fBDirectories\fR
XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_CONFIG_DIRS, XDG_CACHE_HOME and XDG_DATA_HOME name the directory roots; HOME is the base when an XDG variable is unset.
.TP
\fBProxy\fR
HTTPS_PROXY, HTTP_PROXY, ALL_PROXY and NO_PROXY (and the lower\-case spellings) name the proxy, unless [network] proxy is set; a SOCKS URL is refused.
.TP
\fBEditor\fR
VISUAL, then EDITOR, is the editor `config edit` starts.
